WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
Hey, everyone. Welcome to Tesla Short's Time Daily episode 438.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.960
It's April 16th, 2026 and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.360
Here's what you need to know about Tesla today.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.480
Let's start with a striking video that's been making the rounds online.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.360
A Tesla owner's sentry camera caught the tense moment when the occupants of a Mercedes

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:25.000
Benz electric SUV jumped out and ran just seconds before their vehicle erupted in flames.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.140
The footage is raw and dramatic.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.640
You see the doors fly open, people sprinting away, and then the fire takes hold.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.520
What makes it especially notable is that it wasn't a Tesla on fire.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.360
Yet the only reason we have such clear documentation is because of a Tesla parked nearby

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.280
that was quietly recording the whole time.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.820
This incident shines a light on how differently electric vehicle safety events get

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.960
captured and discussed depending on the brand.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.140
Tesla vehicles with their always on camera systems create this constant stream of real

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.320
world evidence that other manufacturers simply don't match.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.260
It's a double-edged sword for the industry.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.140
On one hand, it gives Tesla owners an incredible tool for personal security and insurance

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:09.060
claims. On the other, it means Tesla branded footage often becomes the public record

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.760
for incidents that involve other cars entirely.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:17.420
My take is that this reinforces just how foundational the camera hardware has become

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:18.820
in Tesla's ecosystem.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.740
It's not just about full self-driving or parking lot security anymore.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.400
These systems are turning into a de facto black box for the entire EV space,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.360
whether competitors like it or not.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.360
And while thermal events can happen to any high-energy battery pack,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.960
the visibility gap is becoming obvious.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.900
Tesla's approach gives owners and the broader public something close to objective truth

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.460
in moments that used to rely on eyewitness accounts or grainy security footage.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.460
That dramatic clip ties directly into the bigger picture of where Tesla stands

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.720
in the global EV race right now.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.180
The company held on to the top spot in worldwide electric vehicle deliveries

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.460
for the first quarter of 2026.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.540
Even with increasing competition from all sides,

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.940
Tesla's volume advantage remains intact.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:09.400
It's worth remembering that this isn't just about selling more cars than anyone else

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.400
in a given three months.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.720
It's about the cumulative experience Tesla has built in scaling production,

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.900
managing supply chains and iterating on manufacturing processes over more than a decade.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:24.260
At the same time, Chinese researchers are making steady incremental progress

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.200
on solid-state battery technology.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:30.980
These labs have been publishing promising results on higher energy density cells

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.260
that could, in theory, deliver longer range and smaller lighter packages

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:36.820
while improving safety margins.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.300
The promise has been around for years and we're still not at the point

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.900
where solid-state batteries are rolling out of Giga factories at scale.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.740
But the trajectory is clear if those technical hurdles

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.920
around manufacturing consistency, cost and cycle life get solved,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.860
it could reshape the cost and performance equation for the entire industry.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.220
For now, though, Tesla's current leadership rests on its ability

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.660
to scale proven lithium ion production

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.220
and its very real software advantages.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.820
The combination of manufacturing maturity and the ability

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.100
to push over the air updates keeps the company ahead on volume.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.340
This matters because it shows how hardware maturity

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.680
and deep software integration still outweigh laboratory breakthroughs

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.220
when it comes to real-world impact.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.100
You can have the most advanced cell chemistry in a clean room,

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.720
but if you can't produce it reliably at millions of units per year,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.720
it doesn't move the needle for customers or for the business.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.800
Tesla's edge isn't flashy in every quarterly headline,

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:38.020
but it's grounded in systems that actually deliver cars people can buy and drive today.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.760
That balance between current strengths and future leaps brings us naturally

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.840
to some important Silicon news.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.520
Tesla has reached a key development milestone

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.120
with its next-generation AI5 chip.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.460
This isn't just another incremental upgrade.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.840
The chip is aimed squarely at powering the optimist robot fleet

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.680
and feeding the enormous compute demands

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.180
of Tesla's expanding supercomputer clusters.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:05.280
Getting the manufacturing process right at this advanced node is no small feat.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:09.280
It still depends on close, trust-based collaboration with foundry partners

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.300
like Samsung, who are pushing the boundaries of what's possible

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.380
in high-volume semiconductor production.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.080
The milestone is meaningful on multiple levels.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.120
It boosts confidence in Samsung's own semiconductor roadmap

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.060
while giving Tesla a clearer, more predictable path

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.920
to scale both its robotic ambitions and its training infrastructure.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:33.500
What stands out to me is how deliberately Tesla is allocating its most advanced silicon

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.220
across different product lines rather than putting all its eggs in one basket.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:39.660
Instead of chasing a single moonshot,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.840
they're thinking in terms of an ecosystem where AI5 serves both the physical world

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.740
through optimists and the digital world through massive inference clusters.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.940
This focused, almost surgical approach could accelerate progress

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.240
in the areas that matter most for Tesla's long-term growth.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.340
It also reflects a level of systems thinking that's still rare in the auto industry.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:05.000
Most legacy car companies are still struggling

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.620
to integrate basic electronics updates

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.040
while Tesla is already carving up next-generation silicon

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.360
between humanoid robots and autonomous driving stacks.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.440
That kind of strategic clarity feels like a quiet,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.700
but significant competitive advantage.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.920
Speaking of hardware that powers the future,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.380
Tesla just gave owners a new reason to actually use full-self driving every single day.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.880
The company has introduced a feature called FSD streaks

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.600
that quietly tracks how many consecutive days you engage with the system.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.880
It adds light, game-like incentives,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.120
little nudges that reward consistency without being obnoxious about it.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:43.640
On the surface, it feels like a small behavioral tweak,

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.620
but the implications run deeper.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.780
More consistent use should translate into significantly more real-world miles

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:54.520
and those miles remain critical fuel for improving the neural networks.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:59.200
Every engaged drive generates labeled data that can be fed back into training loops.

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.700
At the same time, I think it's fair to raise legitimate questions

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:07.780
about whether safety critical software should lean on behavioral nudges and gamification.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.780
There's a delicate balance here.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.020
More data clearly speeds up development,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.520
but drivers still need to stay responsible, alert, and ready to take over

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.260
even when the system is performing well.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.140
I see both sides pretty clearly.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.360
The feature feels like smart product design,

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.820
but it shouldn't ever replace the fundamental expectation that autonomy is still supervised.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:29.300
That extra stream of consistent, full-self driving data

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.200
could become even more valuable thanks to another quiet,

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.300
but genuinely useful update in the Tesla mobile app.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.340
The latest version now automatically embeds rich metadata

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.340
whenever you download sentry mode or dashcam clips.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.920
Things like vehicle speeds, steering wheel angle, pedal inputs,

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.800
and whether full-self driving was engaged at the time

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:49.340
are all baked right into the file.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:53.040
No more hunting through logs or trying to remember context after the fact.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.240
This small change makes it substantially easier for owners

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.320
to keep accurate personal records or share properly contextualized footage

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:04.480
with insurance companies, police, or even other drivers involved in an incident.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:09.560
It further strengthens the everyday usefulness of Tesla's always-on camera system,

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.160
turning what used to be a security feature into a comprehensive documentation tool

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.520
for protection, claims, and even just personal peace of mind.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.560
From a pure product experience standpoint,

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.240
these kinds of thoughtful, low-key improvements

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:28.160
are exactly what keep the Tesla ownership experience feeling a step ahead

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.580
of everything else on the road.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:35.180
And of course, those same cameras were responsible for capturing the Mercedes fire we started with.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.840
The footage has spread widely because it's dramatic,

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:41.760
but also because it highlights how EV incidents are documented

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.560
and perceived differently depending on who made the vehicle.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:49.600
For Tesla, it quietly reinforces the dual value of its camera systems

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.840
as both personal safety tools for owners and rich,

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:56.840
time-stamped data sources that can benefit the broader industry.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:01.940
It's a reminder that while battery thermal events can happen across manufacturers,

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:07.220
the always-recording nature of Tesla vehicles provides a level of transparency that's still unique.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:11.220
While one legacy electric model is effectively exiting the stage,

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:14.180
Tesla continues to fight older battles on the sales side.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:19.720
BMW has confirmed it will stop selling the IX Electric SUV in the United States

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:21.760
after the 2026 model year.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:24.660
The company is shifting its focus toward its newer,

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:29.700
new-class platform, starting with the smaller IX3 that's expected to arrive later.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:34.860
This decision feels like a pragmatic pruning of early-generation electric models

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.560
that simply haven't hit the volume or cost targets the company needs.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.220
It leaves a bit more breathing room in the premium SUV segment

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:45.960
for Tesla's refreshed Model S and Model X at least in the short term.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:50.940
The move also underscores how challenging it remains for traditional manufacturers

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.820
to sustain multiple distinct electric platforms at true scale.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.300
Developing one competitive EV architecture is hard enough.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:04.160
Trying to keep several generations alive while investing in the next one stretches resources thin.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:08.800
Tesla's decision years ago to focus on a unified platform approach.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.480
Even as it evolves the cells and the software on top of it looks even smarter in that light.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:17.820
Tesla's own premium halo cars are creating a different kind of friction at the moment.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.160
The company is imposing significant financial penalties on buyers

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:26.660
who flip certain limited edition Model S and Model X vehicles shortly after taking delivery.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.760
Reports suggest these fines can climb into the tens of thousands of dollars.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:34.520
The policy is clearly designed to protect allocation fairness

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:37.920
and maintain the exclusive positioning of these high-end models.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:43.920
In an environment where demand for the plaid versions and special editions far outstrips supply,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.360
Tesla doesn't want speculators treating cars like short-term investments.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:53.840
That said, the approach does raise real questions about balancing demand control with basic customer freedom.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:10:00.740
What happens if someone's life circumstances change unexpectedly, a new job, a move, a family situation,

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:02.980
shortly after they finally get their dream car?

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.400
From a pure business standpoint, the intent makes sense.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:10.600
Tesla wants these halo vehicles in the hands of genuine enthusiasts,

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:13.760
not flippers driving up secondary market prices,

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:18.700
but the execution risks creating unnecessary tension and bad feelings among buyers

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.800
who feel they're being punished for circumstances beyond their control.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:26.880
It's a tough line to walk and I suspect we'll see how it plays out over the next few quarters.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.180
That tension between control and customer goodwill actually leads into something deeper

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.740
about how Tesla is weaving its various products together.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.520
A lot of people still look at Tesla insurance, full self-driving,

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:43.000
and the Dojo compute efforts as somewhat separate side businesses.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:48.900
In reality, they're forming a single tightening feedback loop that gets more powerful with every mile driven.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.340
Every insured, full self-driving mile generates both direct revenue and precisely labeled training data.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:59.540
That data improves the software which in turn reduces claims,

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:03.880
tightens the risk model and ultimately lowers insurance rates for safe drivers.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:08.220
By owning the entire stack from the cameras to the inference engines,

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.700
the training clusters, the actuarial tables, and the final pricing,

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:17.260
Tesla removes multiple layers of intermediaries that traditional automakers and insurers have to deal with.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:23.240
Competitors can't easily copy this because it requires end-to-end control over the entire critical data path.

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:27.960
From first principles, you simply cannot optimize what you do not fully control.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:32.860
That integrated approach is quietly becoming one of Tesla's hardest advantages to replicate

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:36.800
and it compounds with scale in a way that's difficult to overstate.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:43.760
This integrated philosophy is exactly why a veteran like Doug Fields recent departure from Ford feels so telling.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:47.760
Field played a major role in ramping the Model 3 production hell at Tesla

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:54.120
then went on to lead Apple's secretive car project before landing at Ford as head of electric vehicles and technology.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.940
He's now left that role amid a broader reorganization at the company.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:02.360
The move highlights the ongoing talent churn inside traditional manufacturers

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:06.700
that are still trying to figure out what software defined vehicles actually mean in practice.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:13.140
Fields experience across Tesla, Apple, and Ford gives him an unusually clear perspective on just how difficult

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:16.680
the cultural and execution gap remains for legacy automakers.

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:22.300
When your background includes shipping hundreds of thousands of cars with continuous software updates

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:26.860
and then you land inside a company that still measures success in model-year cycles

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:32.820
the mismatch can be jarring moves like this often signal deeper challenges around retaining top

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:38.060
technical talent when speed decision making freedom and engineering culture differ so sharply

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:43.840
between the old guard and the new talent movement is one thing but regulatory positioning remains

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:48.780
another persistent battleground Tesla is currently telling regulators in North Dakota that the

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:52.920
company should be classified as a distributor rather than a traditional manufacturer.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:57.980
This is part of its long-running fight against franchise dealer laws that still exist in various

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:03.120
forms across many US states. The direct sales model isn't just a preference for Tesla,

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:07.380
it remains core to the company's ability to deliver rapid software updates,

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:12.940
control pricing strategy, and maintain a consistent customer experience from configuration to delivery

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:19.480
to service. Winning this particular classification battle would allow Tesla to sell vehicles directly

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:23.600
to consumers in North Dakota without having to work through independent dealers.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:29.240
These regulatory fights don't always generate the same excitement as new hardware announcements

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:34.980
but they shape the business environment in fundamental ways. Sometimes the biggest constraints

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:40.180
on Tesla's growth aren't technical at all. Their legal and structural and the company continues

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:44.560
to chip away at them state by state. While fighting those battles in some markets,

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:48.860
Tesla is also using its own insurance product to sweeten the deal in others.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:54.020
Buyers who either subscribe to or purchase full self-driving can now unlock meaningful

00:13:54.020 --> 00:14:00.360
discounts on Tesla insurance in eligible regions. This strategy closes another loop by combining

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:05.660
real driving behavior data with insurance pricing in a way that traditional insurers simply cannot

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:11.100
match. It has the potential to accelerate data collection while simultaneously rewarding drivers who

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:16.240
demonstrate consistently safer behavior behind the wheel. The mutual reinforcement here is elegant.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:21.660
Better software leads to safer driving, which leads to fewer claims, which leads to better insurance

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:26.660
rates, which encourages more people to use the software. It's the same integrated thinking we see

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:31.860
across so many parts of the business, and it's the kind of flywheel that becomes more powerful the

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:36.860
longer it spins. All of this circles back in a way to how valuable even the earliest Tesla's

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:44.000
remain more than a decade later. One enthusiast recently picked up a 2013 Model S at what sounds like

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:48.960
an exceptionally low price on the used market. He's been posting about how he still gets compliments

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:55.000
on the car almost everywhere he goes. People are drawn to the distinctive shape, the quiet ride,

00:14:55.460 --> 00:15:01.540
and the sense of history the car carries. The story nicely illustrates the durable appeal and surprisingly

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:06.620
strong residual value that even the very first Tesla's still command in certain circles.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:12.420
It's genuinely encouraging to see vehicles from more than 10 years ago still turning heads and

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:17.640
delivering satisfying ownership experiences. That kind of longevity speaks to some of the

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:22.820
fundamental engineering choices made right at the beginning, choices around the large central touch screen,

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:29.140
the over-the-air update capability, and the focus on a minimalist interior that has aged better

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:35.240
than many expected. Not every decision from that era was perfect, but the core bet on software and

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:41.220
a clean user interface has held up remarkably well. Now for a couple of other developments that are

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:46.880
generating conversation across the owner community, many people are getting their first real look at

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:52.780
Groc-powered reminders integrated into the latest software update. Instead of the usual rigid voice

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:58.460
commands, the system now lets you set location-based or time-based reminders in a much more natural

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:03.980
conversational way. Early feedback suggests it makes the in- cabin experience feel noticeably

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:08.300
smarter for everyday tasks, like remembering to pick up groceries when you're near the store,

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:13.420
or setting a reminder to call someone when you get home. It's a small step, but it feels like a

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:18.840
meaningful one toward making the car's artificial intelligence genuinely useful in daily life rather

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:24.060
than just a novelty. These kinds of refinements matter because they slowly shift the perception of what

00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:29.500
cars computer can actually do for you beyond driving. Separately, Tesla has opened the submission

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:35.300
portal for shareholder questions ahead of next week's earnings call. This process almost always surfaces

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:41.400
detailed, sometimes pointed inquiries on everything from robotaxi timelines to energy storage margins

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:46.820
to capital allocation discipline. Given the recent movement in the stock and the intense focus on

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:51.860
autonomy, I'd expect plenty of questions around full self-driving adoption metrics and how the company

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:56.600
is thinking about balancing near-term spending with long-term bets on optimists and compute.

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:02.840
Elon Musk also took time to clarify that the AI-5 hardware is aimed squarely at optimist robots,

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:08.460
and the largest supercomputer clusters, while suggesting that the current AI-4 generation should be

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:13.640
more than sufficient to reach better than human safety levels for full self-driving. That distinction

